    {
      "id": "cert-lv/spicy-iec104",
      "url": "https://github.com/cert-lv/spicy-iec104/tree/e758272584b063a4789899db19be7d56a4f665c3/testing/Traces",
      "revision": "e758272584b063a4789899db19be7d56a4f665c3",
      "terms": "The repository is under the BSD 3-Clause licence (COPYING, Copyright 2023 Georgios Michail Makrakis, 2024 CERT.LV); the traces have no statement of their own.",
      "notes": "Test traces of CERT.LV's Spicy/Zeek IEC 104 analyzer. first/iec104.pcap is a copy of https://github.com/automayt/ICS-pcap/blob/master/IEC%2060870/iec104/iec104.pcap (testing/Traces/first/README); fifth/ and sixth/ were added in 2025 (commits 344fe86, b06d12f) without a statement of how they were made. second/, third/ and fourth/ are byte-identical copies of files of the Zenodo record and are fetched by make captures-fetch, not committed. testing/Baseline holds Zeek's logs of the traces."
    },
    {
      "id": "zenodo-7108614",
      "url": "https://zenodo.org/records/7108614",
      "revision": "record 7108614, revision 6 (published 2022-09-23, updated 2024-07-16)",
      "terms": "CC-BY-4.0 (record metadata). Cite: P. Radoglou-Grammatikis et al., IEEE Transactions on Industrial Informatics 18(3), 2022, doi:10.1109/TII.2021.3093905.",
      "notes": "IEC 60870-5-104 Intrusion Detection Dataset, University of Western Macedonia. Each 7z archive holds, per entity, the whole traffic and an *_iec104_only.pcap filtered to the IEC 104 frames (no SYN, no ACK-only segments). The ReadMe names five virtual RTUs (IEC TestServer on Windows 7), two real RTU devices, QTester104 v2.1 as HMI and three Kali Linux attackers (Metasploit iec104 module, OpenMUC j60870). The ReadMe identifies the real RTUs neither by address nor by make or model. In the recordings all seven controlled stations (192.168.1.13, .19, .21, .22, .24, .25, .26) have MAC addresses of the VirtualBox range 08:00:27 and behave identically; none can be shown to be a physical device."
    },
